{"title":"2019 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 7","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2019 AMC 10A Problems\">2019 AMC 10A #7</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_12A_Problems\" title=\"2019 AMC 12A Problems\">2019 AMC 12A #5</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_9_(Trig)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9 (Trig)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Solution_10\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 10</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Solution_11_(Heron&#39;s_Formula)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 11 (Heron's Formula)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Solution_12\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 12</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Two lines with slopes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/4/4a4a7df1100efe353cc612d5d1c289c22d5bf8c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> intersect at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/b/b2b596a120dcf60e33c7bfe19003bb1675bf37a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. What is the area of the triangle enclosed by these two lines and the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/4/cc4a4b19609ae5d83671cd2b2803a3781178d3c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y=10  ?$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"92\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/f/b9f0333d6edd5e3ce079fc971dd9e1d25de88b63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 4 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 4\\sqrt{2} \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 6 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 8 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 6\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"402\" height=\"21\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's first work out the slope-intercept form of all three lines:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/8/ff811a70e36e6b6f5be8a11a0162dd987fd51b26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x,y)=(2,2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/d/68d3046ad3a93ec75bc68a1242e81c1f17bf3599.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\frac{x}{2} + b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"78\" height=\"33\" /> implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/4/6e476ea6819fd8832851935bef3b4fc9539893b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2=\\frac{2}{2} +b=&gt; 2=1+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"189\" height=\"38\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/5/cf590e9abb33aa9776ddc386b1ba6ee7b43fa413.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"40\" height=\"13\" />, while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e1261eef3a40ffc3c46fa8b5a6e95894472b2aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=2x + c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" /> implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/0/6f0aca194f46e8ac926552128f8c0ea3b3be6e9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2= 2 \\cdot 2+c=&gt; 2=4+c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"207\" height=\"15\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/5/52566cda76e94a472e3d567b038aff381ea43375.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=-2$\" width=\"54\" height=\"12\" />. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/3/6631d7f1b7fd1489eb9406d34ae9566aa2a796db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y=10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"84\" height=\"16\" /> implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/9/5692f7cbcbd5ab2ab9a4d5e60319152f275aca19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=-x+10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"16\" />. Thus the lines are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/73495636fd3674e97c4e36a4a0da969f9c18502b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=\\frac{x}{2} +1, y=2x-2,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"176\" height=\"33\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/9/5692f7cbcbd5ab2ab9a4d5e60319152f275aca19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=-x+10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"16\" />. \nNow we find the intersection points between each of the lines with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/9/5692f7cbcbd5ab2ab9a4d5e60319152f275aca19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=-x+10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"16\" />, which are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/d/07d9cf67e797afa7e029e4b13c4c758197f8cfd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/5/0c5d8e12531fe9f78ed85df73d0591e676489292.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4,6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. Using the distance formula and then the Pythagorean Theorem, we see that we have an isosceles triangle with base <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/d/71df1b44d6caa4b0f798d48ff8ee72da386e4cb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> and height <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/5/005458b4333cedea7a3aa771f6620c976597f9af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />, whose area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/4/394e31b6e19ba6e9c14f340669915c86d068a16a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in the other solutions, solve the systems of equations to see that the triangle's two other vertices are at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. Then apply Heron's Formula: the semi-perimeter will be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/f/8cf154c4864628d14e931c9183e9d0fb4d2d74a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{20}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"113\" height=\"18\" />, so the area reduces nicely to a difference of squares, making it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/4/394e31b6e19ba6e9c14f340669915c86d068a16a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in the other solutions, we find, either using algebra or simply by drawing the lines on squared paper, that the three points of intersection are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. We can now draw the bounding square with vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/f/70f47290d3c991aac723270e49b5415eb82b2cc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/d/0dd1499dbb1ea032542c13537a2ca2f157d4c832.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/7/c17384a6c2a4aee0ea47051a974ad1cb5967d47f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and deduce that the triangle's area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/e/82e95e9c88d8f6118f4b88c4c1465732e25d1bce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16-4-2-4=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"192\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find that the three points of intersection are  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. Using graph paper, we can see that this triangle has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> boundary lattice points and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> interior lattice points. By Pick's Theorem, the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/d/27d86049c0935086ef2e7f73e6e037bcd51e66b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac62 + 4 - 1 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"155\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find the three points of intersection. Label these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcfdc67f6d45715b20189ec86501becf1d0df62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A (2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52f7793a83ec02654dae53afbfdbfae4c20d8f5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B (4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/6/8d6649773c4de1502ee0efbe1501a0a0f909b37a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C (6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/4/fc424bebcedbab35f81f9f91fbfeabaadff6a874.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = AC = 2\\sqrt5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"138\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e049f11dc669c8d0ed3f837af986ff08bb59fda6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = 2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" />. By the Law of Cosines,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/892e5ef8e9bb6a7e267aa95a08a4586a1ba2c091.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos A = \\frac{(2\\sqrt5)^2 + (2\\sqrt5)^2 - (2\\sqrt2)^2}{2 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5} = \\frac{20 + 20 - 8}{40} = \\frac{32}{40} = \\frac45\\]\" width=\"478\" height=\"45\" />\nTherefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/d/78dcae3f11ffbf5c754dc3b9824227f50b4ad883.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin A = \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 A} = \\frac35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"194\" height=\"37\" />, so the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/0/530f7bdd44146fc31be989839b8a623b70ffe31b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12 bc \\sin A = \\frac12 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5 \\cdot \\frac35 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"307\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find that the three points of intersection are  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. The area of the triangle is half the absolute value of the determinant of the matrix determined by these points.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f994ffa1b84dc290029c4169e1a9ce9026b24c4c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac12\\begin{Vmatrix} 2&amp;2&amp;1\\\\ 4&amp;6&amp;1\\\\ 6&amp;4&amp;1\\\\ \\end{Vmatrix} = \\frac12|-12| = \\frac12 \\cdot 12 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}\\]\" width=\"331\" height=\"65\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7\">Solution 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find the three points of intersection. Label these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcfdc67f6d45715b20189ec86501becf1d0df62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A (2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52f7793a83ec02654dae53afbfdbfae4c20d8f5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B (4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/6/8d6649773c4de1502ee0efbe1501a0a0f909b37a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C (6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />. Then vectors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/3/9c3985e33e2dd5e12ff973b29ab5ae31ade7ac6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\langle 2, 4 \\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"91\" height=\"27\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/5/a25ed1cf7eeb26dd1b5fc052e11b07e40bccf8dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overrightarrow{AC} = \\langle 4, 2 \\rangle$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"25\" />. The area of the triangle is half the magnitude of the cross product of these two vectors.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e1039eb07fa740e731a0bb2a1c057f99418b69c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac12\\begin{Vmatrix} i&amp;j&amp;k\\\\ 2&amp;4&amp;0\\\\ 4&amp;2&amp;0\\\\ \\end{Vmatrix} = \\frac12|-12k| = \\frac12 \\cdot 12 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}\\]\" width=\"342\" height=\"65\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8\">Solution 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find that the three points of intersection are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. By the Pythagorean theorem, this is an isosceles triangle with base <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4091ce096ade72ac87c96d6653144cb69b829e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> and equal length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/b/f8b54fd6da34b12c5b556d39b7cb25ac84524a1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. The area of an isosceles triangle with base <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> and equal length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/2/9b25f8e64b484493fda944d25cad453423041fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l$\" width=\"5\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/5/15565a4acc0c0fe3413b7e5fd202fa324ab15e54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{b\\sqrt{4l^2-b^2}}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"85\" height=\"41\" />. Plugging  in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/c/70c7a1c83d86a202ed4a8b47148960f4e03602a0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b = 2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/7/087a79a89a755711aab8b9671be7dd99ee4619c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l = 2\\sqrt5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be69f5c05a7af231d373da9a94fa7edeafa3558b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2\\sqrt2 \\cdot \\sqrt{80-8}}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{576}}{4} = \\frac{24}{4} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}\\]\" width=\"310\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_9_.28Trig.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9_(Trig)\">Solution 9 (Trig)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9 (Trig)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find the three points of intersection. Label these <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/c/2bcfdc67f6d45715b20189ec86501becf1d0df62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A (2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52f7793a83ec02654dae53afbfdbfae4c20d8f5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B (4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/6/8d6649773c4de1502ee0efbe1501a0a0f909b37a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C (6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />. By the Pythagorean Theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/4/fc424bebcedbab35f81f9f91fbfeabaadff6a874.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = AC = 2\\sqrt5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"138\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e049f11dc669c8d0ed3f837af986ff08bb59fda6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC = 2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" />. By the Law of Cosines,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/2/892e5ef8e9bb6a7e267aa95a08a4586a1ba2c091.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\cos A = \\frac{(2\\sqrt5)^2 + (2\\sqrt5)^2 - (2\\sqrt2)^2}{2 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5} = \\frac{20 + 20 - 8}{40} = \\frac{32}{40} = \\frac45\\]\" width=\"478\" height=\"45\" />\nTherefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/d/78dcae3f11ffbf5c754dc3b9824227f50b4ad883.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin A = \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^2 A} = \\frac35$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"194\" height=\"37\" />. By the extended Law of Sines,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/d/55d8b24a51e26fc3c78205abaee1287ad4b4f5c5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2R = \\frac{a}{\\sin A} = \\frac{2\\sqrt2}{\\frac35} = \\frac{10\\sqrt2}{3}\\]\" width=\"220\" height=\"47\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49ee1bac99fe6812a6d322311d0cc2f25120c6eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R = \\frac{5\\sqrt2}{3}\\]\" width=\"73\" height=\"41\" />\nThen the area is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/591e87ab2b7418096ba8113dd01b49887d7efd9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{abc}{4R} = \\frac{2\\sqrt2 \\cdot 2\\sqrt5^2}{4 \\cdot \\frac{5\\sqrt2}{3}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"224\" height=\"54\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_10\">Solution 10</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 10\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The area of a triangle formed by three lines,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/2/c5213152a098240921ec897aa9535ebbddf46937.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_1x + a_2y + a_3 = 0\\]\" width=\"148\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/5/fc5053d6f01b30bc24109992b7c171c7b8cf1998.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b_1x + b_2y + b_3 = 0\\]\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/5/c95182b7dfae32e1dcb8d287bd0d7aca7d55bc80.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[c_1x + c_2y + c_3 = 0\\]\" width=\"142\" height=\"16\" />\nis the absolute value of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/7/e7740f053e3a95ec9d8b8d88dfc70a61f0762d19.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{(b_1c_2-b_2c_1)(a_1c_2-a_2c_1)(a_1b_2-a_2b_1)} \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} a_1&amp;a_2&amp;a_3\\\\ b_1&amp;b_2&amp;b_3\\\\ c_1&amp;c_2&amp;c_3\\\\ \\end{vmatrix}^2\\]\" width=\"443\" height=\"67\" />\nPlugging in the three lines,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/9/f795e00ed9939ddc81f976007948b3dad063aa35.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-x + 2y - 2 = 0\\]\" width=\"128\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/5/f456564850021a5521942b7c1e713b6dbfba59d0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[-2x + y + 2 = 0\\]\" width=\"128\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/5/115ed18897ba2bd6cc302b0b459a1da8a306209b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + y - 10 = 0\\]\" width=\"115\" height=\"16\" />\nthe area is the absolute value of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/4/284c523ba6d5806998b5ef494faa213bacc2c43e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{(-2-1)(-1-2)(-1+4)} \\cdot \\begin{vmatrix} -1&amp;2&amp;-2\\\\ -2&amp;1&amp;2\\\\ 1&amp;1&amp;-10\\\\ \\end{vmatrix}^2 = \\frac12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{27} \\cdot 18^2 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}\\]\" width=\"523\" height=\"67\" />\nSource: Orrick, Michael L. “THE AREA OF A TRIANGLE FORMED BY THREE LINES.” Pi Mu Epsilon Journal, vol. 7, no. 5, 1981, pp. 294–298. JSTOR, www.jstor.org/stable/24336991.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_11_.28Heron.27s_Formula.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_11_(Heron's_Formula)\">Solution 11 (Heron's Formula)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 11 (Heron&#39;s Formula)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in other solutions, we find that our triangle is isosceles with legs of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/b/f8b54fd6da34b12c5b556d39b7cb25ac84524a1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> and base <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4091ce096ade72ac87c96d6653144cb69b829e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. Then, the semi - perimeter of our triangle is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/f/d7f54639dcf0b3001483c4e645e9d62a7678f3f0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4\\sqrt5+2\\sqrt2}{2} = 2\\sqrt5 + \\sqrt2.\\]\" width=\"198\" height=\"41\" /> Applying Heron's formula, we find that the area of this triangle is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/4/d2450d5287dead8b450405a064d85614aa44ebb5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{{(2\\sqrt5+\\sqrt2)}{(2\\sqrt5-\\sqrt2)}{(2)}} = \\sqrt{{(20-2)}{(2)}} = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}.\\]\" width=\"439\" height=\"34\" />\n</p><p>~rbcubed13\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_12\">Solution 12</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 12\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like in the other solutions, we find that the three points of intersection are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5506b58320deb5004bf2ed2f1a5e3e672d6e326e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2, 2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/0/190d53edddf17fdf15d88488290c282a150354d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(4, 6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/a/50a6aa4fae2c4e4c29b43f57a15cd214e525e111.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(6, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />. By the shoelace theorem, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/4/f94b65ef3ea2b334b1e071f635efc860a7c61edd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left|(2*4 + 6*6 + 4*2) - (2*6 + 4*4 + 6*2) \\right|\\]\" width=\"373\" height=\"37\" /> Evaluating this we get our answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/9/8e981cbb7afc69f571c073b25c41e660f6bb1710.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"62\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~tontongoated110\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1\">Video Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/KWs9FpLSi5A\">https://youtu.be/KWs9FpLSi5A</a>\n</p><p>Education, the Study of Everything\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2\">Video Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2019_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Ed